Aim:
To handle layout managers in Java and to build a small form using swing controls with an event listener.
9(a) Handling Layouts
Program 9.1 — BorderDemo.java (17 lines)
// Ex 9(a): BorderLayout - five regions
import javax.swing.*;
import java.awt.*;
public class BorderDemo {
public static void main(String[] args) {
JFrame f = new JFrame("BorderLayout");
f.setLayout(new BorderLayout());
f.add(new JButton("NORTH"), BorderLayout.NORTH);
f.add(new JButton("SOUTH"), BorderLayout.SOUTH);
f.add(new JButton("EAST"), BorderLayout.EAST);
f.add(new JButton("WEST"), BorderLayout.WEST);
f.add(new JButton("CENTER"), BorderLayout.CENTER);
f.setSize(350, 220);
f.setDefaultCloseOperation(JFrame.EXIT_ON_CLOSE);
f.setVisible(true);
}
}.
Program 9.2 — GridDemo.java (17 lines)
// Ex 9(a): GridLayout - a 4 x 3 keypad, all boxes equal
import javax.swing.*;


SCSVMV University | Department of Computer Science and Engineering | Java Programming Laboratory

import java.awt.*;
public class GridDemo {
public static void main(String[] args) {
JFrame f = new JFrame("GridLayout 4 x 3");
f.setLayout(new GridLayout(4, 3, 5, 5));
String[] keys = { "1","2","3","4","5","6","7","8","9","*","0","#" };
for (String k : keys)
f.add(new JButton(k));
f.setSize(260, 300);
f.setDefaultCloseOperation(JFrame.EXIT_ON_CLOSE);
f.setVisible(true);
}
}
Program 9.3 — FlowDemo.java (16 lines)
// Ex 9(a): FlowLayout - a row that wraps
import javax.swing.*;
import java.awt.*;
public class FlowDemo {
public static void main(String[] args) {
JFrame f = new JFrame("FlowLayout");
f.setLayout(new FlowLayout());
String[] b = { "New", "Open", "Save", "Print", "Exit" };
for (String s : b)
f.add(new JButton(s));
f.setSize(300, 120);
f.setDefaultCloseOperation(JFrame.EXIT_ON_CLOSE);
f.setVisible(true);
}
}
Output
BorderDemo : NORTH across the top, SOUTH across the bottom,
WEST and EAST at the sides, CENTER fills the middle.
Enlarge the window - only CENTER grows.
GridDemo : twelve equal keys in four rows of three.
FlowDemo : five buttons in a row; narrow the window and
they wrap onto a second row.
9(b) Swing Controls
Algorithm
1. Start.
2. Create a JFrame and set FlowLayout.
3. Create a JTextField for the user name and a JPasswordField for the password.





4. Create a JButton and a JLabel that will show the message.
5. Add all the controls to the frame.
6. Attach an ActionListener to the button that checks the two values.
7. Set the size, the close operation and make the frame visible.
8. Stop.
Program 9.4 — LoginForm.java (25 lines)
// Ex 9(b): swing controls with an event listener
import javax.swing.*;
import java.awt.*;
public class LoginForm {
public static void main(String[] args) {
JFrame f = new JFrame("Login");
f.setLayout(new FlowLayout());
JTextField user = new JTextField(12);
JPasswordField pass = new JPasswordField(12);
JButton ok = new JButton("Login");
JLabel msg = new JLabel(" ");
f.add(new JLabel("User Name")); f.add(user);
f.add(new JLabel("Password ")); f.add(pass);
f.add(ok);
f.add(msg);
ok.addActionListener(e -> {
String pwd = new String(pass.getPassword());
if (user.getText().equals("cse") && pwd.equals("java"))
msg.setText("Login successful");
else
msg.setText("Invalid user or password");
});
f.setSize(230, 180);
f.setDefaultCloseOperation(JFrame.EXIT_ON_CLOSE);
f.setVisible(true);
}
}
Output
+---------------------------+
| User Name [ cse ] |
| Password [ **** ] |
| [ Login ] |
| Login successful |
+---------------------------+
Typing anything else gives: Invalid user or password
Case Study 9 — Student Registration Form
Problem. Build a form with a name field, a branch drop-down, a gender choice and a Submit button. When
Submit is pressed, show the entered details, and refuse to submit if the name is empty.
Solution — RegisterForm.java (25 lines)
// Case Study 9: form with validation
import javax.swing.*;
import java.awt.*;





public class RegisterForm {
public static void main(String[] args) {
JFrame f = new JFrame("Student Registration");
f.setLayout(new FlowLayout());
JTextField name = new JTextField(14);
JComboBox<String> br = new JComboBox<>(new String[]{"CSE","IT","ECE"});
JRadioButton m = new JRadioButton("Male", true), fe = new JRadioButton("Female");
ButtonGroup g = new ButtonGroup(); g.add(m); g.add(fe);
JButton sub = new JButton("Submit");
JLabel out = new JLabel(" ");
f.add(new JLabel("Name")); f.add(name);
f.add(new JLabel("Branch")); f.add(br);
f.add(m); f.add(fe); f.add(sub); f.add(out);
sub.addActionListener(e -> {
if (name.getText().trim().isEmpty())
JOptionPane.showMessageDialog(f, "Name cannot be empty");
else
out.setText(name.getText() + " | " + br.getSelectedItem()
+ " | " + (m.isSelected() ? "Male" : "Female"));
});
f.setSize(300, 220); f.setDefaultCloseOperation(JFrame.EXIT_ON_CLOSE);
f.setVisible(true);
}
}
Output
Name [ Divya Lakshmi ]
Branch [ CSE v ]
(o) Male ( ) Female
[ Submit ]
Divya Lakshmi | CSE | Female
With an empty name, a dialog box appears: "Name cannot be empty"
Result
Layout managers and swing controls with event handling were implemented and a validated registration form
was executed successfully.
